1
00:00:00,862 --> 00:00:03,082
Last episode we built a knowledge base,

2
00:00:03,162 --> 00:00:04,649
and we deliberately did not write a

3
00:00:04,649 --> 00:00:07,794
rulebook. This episode fills that in.

4
00:00:08,537 --> 00:00:11,662
Two questions. What a rulebook actually

5
00:00:11,662 --> 00:00:13,074
is, and how it comes out

6
00:00:13,074 --> 00:00:14,244
of the work itself.

7
00:00:15,617 --> 00:00:17,992
This episode has sixteen steps, in three

8
00:00:17,992 --> 00:00:20,830
parts. In part one we get the terms

9
00:00:20,830 --> 00:00:22,717
straight, then lay the rules out one at a

10
00:00:22,717 --> 00:00:26,030
time. In part two we decide which rules to

11
00:00:26,030 --> 00:00:28,242
write now and which to leave alone, and

12
00:00:28,242 --> 00:00:31,255
then we write them. In part three we take

13
00:00:31,255 --> 00:00:33,637
five papers and test the rulebook on them.

14
00:00:35,005 --> 00:00:38,542
Step one. Ask the AI what Claude.md

15
00:00:38,542 --> 00:00:41,675
actually is. The answer is simple.

16
00:00:42,417 --> 00:00:44,430
It is a plain text file, and there is

17
00:00:44,430 --> 00:00:46,350
nothing special about the format.

18
00:00:47,092 --> 00:00:48,962
The only special thing is the name.

19
00:00:49,705 --> 00:00:51,705
Every time the AI starts up it goes

20
00:00:51,705 --> 00:00:53,792
looking for that file, and treats what is

21
00:00:53,792 --> 00:00:55,855
inside as standing instructions for this

22
00:00:55,855 --> 00:00:58,730
project. Look at the last column of the

23
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:01,675
table on screen. When it loads.

24
00:01:02,417 --> 00:01:04,037
That column matters later.

25
00:01:05,408 --> 00:01:07,471
One thing has to be clear before anything

26
00:01:07,471 --> 00:01:10,896
else. The content of Claude.md is not

27
00:01:10,896 --> 00:01:13,471
executed. It is pasted into the

28
00:01:13,471 --> 00:01:16,496
conversation. The effect is the same as

29
00:01:16,496 --> 00:01:18,308
pasting it by hand at the start of every

30
00:01:18,308 --> 00:01:22,191
session. So, first, it has no force.

31
00:01:22,933 --> 00:01:24,696
A rule written vaguely is a rule not

32
00:01:24,696 --> 00:01:27,596
written. Second, you pay for it on every

33
00:01:27,596 --> 00:01:29,878
turn, even to ask a small question.

34
00:01:30,621 --> 00:01:33,028
Third, too many rules dilute each other.

35
00:01:33,771 --> 00:01:35,946
Every judgment later in this episode comes

36
00:01:35,946 --> 00:01:37,266
from those three points.

37
00:01:38,644 --> 00:01:42,207
Step two. Ask the AI to list the rules for

38
00:01:42,207 --> 00:01:45,257
writing an entry. The table it gives back

39
00:01:45,257 --> 00:01:48,257
has an extra column. Where each rule came

40
00:01:48,257 --> 00:01:51,194
from. Something you gave it, or something

41
00:01:51,194 --> 00:01:53,957
we hit the hard way. That column is the

42
00:01:53,957 --> 00:01:55,514
method of this whole episode.

43
00:01:56,257 --> 00:01:58,557
Last episode a plan was overruled with one

44
00:01:58,557 --> 00:02:00,819
word, scrap, and the problem was not the

45
00:02:00,819 --> 00:02:03,614
content. It had no source.

46
00:02:04,357 --> 00:02:07,507
It was guesswork. Once every rule has to

47
00:02:07,507 --> 00:02:10,056
name a source, why a rule exists becomes

48
00:02:10,056 --> 00:02:11,327
something you can check.

49
00:02:12,696 --> 00:02:14,078
The list keeps going.

50
00:02:14,821 --> 00:02:16,846
Which fields the header needs, and what

51
00:02:16,846 --> 00:02:18,328
order the page is written in.

52
00:02:19,071 --> 00:02:21,041
One line here is worth remembering.

53
00:02:21,783 --> 00:02:24,721
Add it on day one. With a single entry

54
00:02:24,721 --> 00:02:26,383
those header fields look like pure

55
00:02:26,383 --> 00:02:29,183
overhead. But if you only want them after

56
00:02:29,183 --> 00:02:31,471
two hundred entries, you go back and edit

57
00:02:31,471 --> 00:02:32,703
two hundred files.

58
00:02:34,075 --> 00:02:35,863
The writing rules and the figure rules

59
00:02:35,863 --> 00:02:38,863
work the same way. Every one carries its

60
00:02:38,863 --> 00:02:41,245
reason. Take this one.

61
00:02:41,988 --> 00:02:44,538
Always turn a table into plain text, never

62
00:02:44,538 --> 00:02:47,100
screenshot it. The reason is that

63
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,350
comparing numbers across papers depends on

64
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:52,583
exactly that. With the reason attached,

65
00:02:52,625 --> 00:02:54,175
when you meet a table too messy to

66
00:02:54,175 --> 00:02:56,088
convert, you know whether you can make an

67
00:02:56,088 --> 00:02:59,263
exception. A rule with no reason is either

68
00:02:59,263 --> 00:03:00,970
obeyed blindly or ignored.

69
00:03:02,343 --> 00:03:03,913
That is twenty-odd rules.

70
00:03:04,655 --> 00:03:06,588
Now do not rush to write them down.

71
00:03:07,330 --> 00:03:09,350
We asked a different question first.

72
00:03:10,093 --> 00:03:12,418
Should these go in Claude.md, or should

73
00:03:12,418 --> 00:03:15,788
they become a skill? The answer was, both,

74
00:03:15,793 --> 00:03:17,143
but where you cut the line is what

75
00:03:17,143 --> 00:03:20,105
matters. There is only one difference that

76
00:03:20,105 --> 00:03:22,750
decides it. When it loads.

77
00:03:23,493 --> 00:03:25,800
Claude.md loads at every startup.

78
00:03:26,543 --> 00:03:28,850
A skill loads only when the task needs it,

79
00:03:28,918 --> 00:03:30,763
and it can carry its own scripts.

80
00:03:32,146 --> 00:03:34,459
By that test, the twenty-odd rules split

81
00:03:34,459 --> 00:03:37,496
into two piles. The standing ones go in

82
00:03:37,496 --> 00:03:40,141
Claude.md, about thirty lines.

83
00:03:40,884 --> 00:03:42,734
Who owns which layer, the directory

84
00:03:42,734 --> 00:03:44,946
structure, how the log is written, and

85
00:03:44,946 --> 00:03:46,896
what to do when sources contradict each

86
00:03:46,896 --> 00:03:49,716
other. Plus two that stand out.

87
00:03:50,459 --> 00:03:52,504
Anything time-sensitive gets looked up,

88
00:03:52,609 --> 00:03:54,409
and numbers pulled out of a document are

89
00:03:54,409 --> 00:03:55,841
never trusted blindly.

90
00:03:56,584 --> 00:03:58,416
Why are those two standing rules?

91
00:03:59,159 --> 00:04:00,946
Because they are traps you can hit at any

92
00:04:00,946 --> 00:04:03,504
moment, not only when handling papers.

93
00:04:04,246 --> 00:04:06,634
Everything else, the page structure, how

94
00:04:06,634 --> 00:04:09,184
figures get recorded, the word counts, is

95
00:04:09,184 --> 00:04:11,454
only used when writing a paper entry.

96
00:04:12,196 --> 00:04:13,541
That goes in the skill.

97
00:04:14,910 --> 00:04:16,705
Here is the part people get wrong.

98
00:04:17,447 --> 00:04:19,272
The test is when it loads, not how

99
00:04:19,272 --> 00:04:22,072
important it is. The nine-block page

100
00:04:22,072 --> 00:04:24,497
structure is very important, but you only

101
00:04:24,497 --> 00:04:26,660
need it when writing an entry, so it goes

102
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:29,385
in the skill. Do not trust the numbers

103
00:04:29,385 --> 00:04:31,297
looks trivial, but you hit it reading a

104
00:04:31,297 --> 00:04:33,860
web page or a report, so it stands all the

105
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:36,710
time. Sort by importance and you get

106
00:04:36,710 --> 00:04:37,905
the opposite answer.

107
00:04:39,290 --> 00:04:41,477
Then the AI added a reminder nobody asked

108
00:04:41,477 --> 00:04:44,577
for. This set of rules has only been run

109
00:04:44,577 --> 00:04:47,515
on one paper. Its advice was to write

110
00:04:47,515 --> 00:04:50,252
Claude.md now, because those thirty lines

111
00:04:50,252 --> 00:04:52,702
are general and unlikely to be wrong, but

112
00:04:52,702 --> 00:04:54,765
to hold off on packaging the skill until a

113
00:04:54,765 --> 00:04:56,297
few more papers are done.

114
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:59,015
That is the other half of last episode's

115
00:04:59,015 --> 00:05:01,702
lesson. Last time we learned not to

116
00:05:01,702 --> 00:05:03,110
legislate in advance.

117
00:05:03,852 --> 00:05:05,440
Here we learn that when the evidence is

118
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:06,947
in, you write it down.

119
00:05:08,325 --> 00:05:10,120
Sorted first, then written.

120
00:05:10,863 --> 00:05:12,888
The AI wrote the rulebook, and split the

121
00:05:12,888 --> 00:05:16,000
project in two. The knowledge base on one

122
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,370
side, the teaching material on the other,

123
00:05:18,475 --> 00:05:19,883
each with its own rulebook.

124
00:05:20,625 --> 00:05:22,300
Before that the two were mixed in one

125
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:24,588
folder, which is why it was unclear which

126
00:05:24,588 --> 00:05:26,208
rulebook a rule belonged to.

127
00:05:26,975 --> 00:05:28,938
It also collected the loose tool scripts

128
00:05:28,938 --> 00:05:30,495
into one fixed place.

129
00:05:31,238 --> 00:05:32,950
And it said out loud that there was one

130
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:34,463
step it could not do, and

131
00:05:34,463 --> 00:05:35,820
needed a person for.

132
00:05:37,193 --> 00:05:38,838
Once it is written, check it.

133
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:41,743
The cheapest check there is, is to have it

134
00:05:41,743 --> 00:05:43,688
list the rules and read them back.

135
00:05:44,430 --> 00:05:46,805
Six hard rules on one screen, and you can

136
00:05:46,805 --> 00:05:48,900
see straight away which one is too vague.

137
00:05:49,643 --> 00:05:51,338
This time it was the first one.

138
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:54,293
It said the raw sources are read only, but

139
00:05:54,293 --> 00:05:55,813
it never said which folder.

140
00:05:56,555 --> 00:05:58,580
Add the full path, and the rule can

141
00:05:58,580 --> 00:05:59,825
actually be followed.

142
00:06:01,212 --> 00:06:03,262
The rulebook has been tested on exactly

143
00:06:03,262 --> 00:06:05,970
one paper, so now we test it properly.

144
00:06:06,712 --> 00:06:08,787
We asked the AI to write up the remaining

145
00:06:08,787 --> 00:06:10,420
five papers as entries.

146
00:06:11,162 --> 00:06:13,257
The prompt ends with an extra clause.

147
00:06:13,1000 --> 00:06:16,182
So the writing rules become more settled.

148
00:06:16,925 --> 00:06:19,200
That tells the AI these five are not just

149
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:20,945
output, they are a test.

150
00:06:21,687 --> 00:06:23,850
Watch for which rule gets in the way, and

151
00:06:23,850 --> 00:06:25,745
which one turns out to be redundant.

152
00:06:27,128 --> 00:06:29,073
The result after five papers.

153
00:06:29,815 --> 00:06:31,690
The eight-block structure held all the way

154
00:06:31,690 --> 00:06:34,148
through, including three tools papers.

155
00:06:34,890 --> 00:06:37,415
The rule we expected to break, translating

156
00:06:37,415 --> 00:06:39,848
every caption in full, did not break.

157
00:06:40,590 --> 00:06:42,328
And the new rules that did come out were

158
00:06:42,328 --> 00:06:44,223
all things nobody could have predicted.

159
00:06:45,604 --> 00:06:48,766
Here is one. You open a finished entry and

160
00:06:48,766 --> 00:06:51,354
find a caption that refers to panel a, b

161
00:06:51,354 --> 00:06:53,554
and c, while the figure itself has no

162
00:06:53,554 --> 00:06:54,786
letters on it at all.

163
00:06:55,529 --> 00:06:57,211
The reader cannot match them up.

164
00:06:57,954 --> 00:06:59,729
Those letters live in the text layer of

165
00:06:59,729 --> 00:07:01,766
the PDF, and the tool that pulls the

166
00:07:01,766 --> 00:07:03,974
pictures out only sees the image layer.

167
00:07:04,716 --> 00:07:06,229
Nobody could have thought of this while

168
00:07:06,229 --> 00:07:08,966
writing rules. It surfaced because

169
00:07:08,966 --> 00:07:10,279
somebody actually read one

170
00:07:10,279 --> 00:07:11,549
of the six entries.

171
00:07:12,935 --> 00:07:14,905
You pointed at two bad figures.

172
00:07:15,648 --> 00:07:17,818
The AI did not just fix those two.

173
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:20,423
It wrote a check program and swept all

174
00:07:20,423 --> 00:07:22,668
thirty-one figures in the six papers.

175
00:07:23,410 --> 00:07:24,898
Three of them had no letters in the

176
00:07:24,898 --> 00:07:27,160
original paper either, so it left those

177
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:28,823
images alone and said so in the

178
00:07:28,823 --> 00:07:31,705
interpretation. The lesson here.

179
00:07:32,448 --> 00:07:34,398
Find one mistake, and assume there are

180
00:07:34,398 --> 00:07:35,730
more of the same kind.

181
00:07:37,099 --> 00:07:38,744
One more rule came from you.

182
00:07:39,486 --> 00:07:41,261
The header should be short, and it should

183
00:07:41,261 --> 00:07:43,111
carry a link to the PDF on your own

184
00:07:43,111 --> 00:07:45,949
machine. That one took three versions to

185
00:07:45,949 --> 00:07:48,674
settle. The episode skips the middle

186
00:07:48,674 --> 00:07:50,474
rounds, but it is worth saying that

187
00:07:50,474 --> 00:07:52,686
changing a rule is often not a one-step

188
00:07:52,686 --> 00:07:55,199
job. And notice that the rulebook

189
00:07:55,199 --> 00:07:58,311
co-evolves. The AI finds rules in the

190
00:07:58,311 --> 00:07:59,806
friction of doing the work.

191
00:08:00,549 --> 00:08:02,661
You find rules in the experience of using

192
00:08:02,661 --> 00:08:05,219
it. Both go into the log.

193
00:08:06,590 --> 00:08:08,915
With all six entries approved, we go back

194
00:08:08,915 --> 00:08:12,078
to the rulebook. The AI updated exactly

195
00:08:12,078 --> 00:08:14,298
two places, each with its source.

196
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:17,215
First, do not blindly trust numbers

197
00:08:17,215 --> 00:08:20,073
widened to numbers, symbols and formulas,

198
00:08:20,153 --> 00:08:21,940
because both actually happened in these

199
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:25,140
five papers. A whole batch of minus signs

200
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:27,240
vanished, and one formula came out in

201
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:30,578
pieces. Second, the section that said the

202
00:08:30,578 --> 00:08:33,003
rules are not settled yet, wait for three

203
00:08:33,003 --> 00:08:35,085
or more papers, was replaced.

204
00:08:35,828 --> 00:08:37,360
It now points at the skill.

205
00:08:38,103 --> 00:08:39,890
The other six hard rules were left

206
00:08:39,890 --> 00:08:43,085
untouched. Untouched is a result too.

207
00:08:43,828 --> 00:08:45,235
They passed the test.

208
00:08:46,618 --> 00:08:48,250
Then the skill was packaged.

209
00:08:48,993 --> 00:08:51,493
One spec file, plus copies of the two tool

210
00:08:51,493 --> 00:08:53,543
programs, so the rules and the tools

211
00:08:53,543 --> 00:08:56,105
travel together. Here you added a

212
00:08:56,105 --> 00:08:58,880
reminder. This knowledge base will not

213
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:01,168
stay in one field, so do not tie the rules

214
00:09:01,168 --> 00:09:04,363
down. The AI put in two safeguards.

215
00:09:05,105 --> 00:09:07,193
There are no field-specific rules inside

216
00:09:07,193 --> 00:09:09,080
the skill, and the ban is written out in

217
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:10,288
the rulebook as well.

218
00:09:11,030 --> 00:09:12,825
But it was honest about the limit.

219
00:09:13,568 --> 00:09:15,863
All six papers are from the same field.

220
00:09:16,605 --> 00:09:18,418
The real test comes when a paper from

221
00:09:18,418 --> 00:09:19,788
another field arrives.

222
00:09:21,174 --> 00:09:23,274
When the AI packages something, you read

223
00:09:23,274 --> 00:09:26,319
it yourself. It laid the whole thing out,

224
00:09:26,399 --> 00:09:28,511
with a table of the structure, and every

225
00:09:28,511 --> 00:09:30,186
section tagged with the step whose

226
00:09:30,186 --> 00:09:31,444
friction produced it.

227
00:09:32,186 --> 00:09:34,349
It also listed four points it was unsure

228
00:09:34,349 --> 00:09:36,231
about, where you might overrule it.

229
00:09:36,974 --> 00:09:38,461
When you review, that is where

230
00:09:38,461 --> 00:09:39,731
your attention goes.

231
00:09:41,113 --> 00:09:42,558
How do you trigger a skill?

232
00:09:43,301 --> 00:09:46,396
Two ways. The first is a command.

233
00:09:47,138 --> 00:09:49,846
Slash, ingest paper, then the file.

234
00:09:50,588 --> 00:09:52,096
That forces it to load.

235
00:09:52,838 --> 00:09:54,596
The second is plain language.

236
00:09:55,338 --> 00:09:57,488
You just say, write this paper up as an

237
00:09:57,488 --> 00:10:00,688
entry. The AI compares your task with the

238
00:10:00,688 --> 00:10:02,776
skill's description, and if they match it

239
00:10:02,776 --> 00:10:04,383
loads the skill by itself.

240
00:10:05,126 --> 00:10:06,446
The difference is this.

241
00:10:07,188 --> 00:10:09,383
If you know you want it, type the command.

242
00:10:10,126 --> 00:10:11,538
If you would rather not remember the

243
00:10:11,538 --> 00:10:12,971
name, just say it.

244
00:10:14,349 --> 00:10:18,244
Last question. Claude.md has to be short.

245
00:10:18,986 --> 00:10:21,831
What about a skill? Can it be long?

246
00:10:22,574 --> 00:10:24,736
It can be much longer, and the reason is

247
00:10:24,736 --> 00:10:26,906
not that a skill gets a bigger allowance.

248
00:10:27,649 --> 00:10:29,261
It is that you pay for it at a different

249
00:10:29,261 --> 00:10:32,549
time. Claude.md is in the room on every

250
00:10:32,549 --> 00:10:34,911
turn, so every line has to answer one

251
00:10:34,911 --> 00:10:37,906
question. Is this used in every job?

252
00:10:38,649 --> 00:10:40,561
A skill loads only when you are doing that

253
00:10:40,561 --> 00:10:42,724
one thing, and then the whole document is

254
00:10:42,724 --> 00:10:45,419
the working spec, so all of it gets used.

255
00:10:46,161 --> 00:10:48,474
The same text is a necessary cost at the

256
00:10:48,474 --> 00:10:50,374
right moment, and a waste at the wrong

257
00:10:50,374 --> 00:10:53,061
one. But do not think a skill can be

258
00:10:53,061 --> 00:10:56,119
endless. Dilution still happens.

259
00:10:56,861 --> 00:10:59,424
The goal for Claude.md is that every line

260
00:10:59,424 --> 00:11:02,386
gets executed. The goal for a skill is

261
00:11:02,386 --> 00:11:03,974
that you can finish the job without

262
00:11:03,974 --> 00:11:05,344
looking anything else up.

263
00:11:06,086 --> 00:11:07,594
The first aims at short.

264
00:11:08,336 --> 00:11:09,906
The second aims at enough.

265
00:11:11,272 --> 00:11:12,942
Three things from this episode.

266
00:11:13,685 --> 00:11:15,855
One, every rule needs a source.

267
00:11:16,597 --> 00:11:18,735
If you cannot name one, it is not ready to

268
00:11:18,735 --> 00:11:21,885
be written. Two, where a rule goes depends

269
00:11:21,885 --> 00:11:23,910
on when it loads, not on how important it

270
00:11:23,910 --> 00:11:27,335
is. Three, once written, test it against

271
00:11:27,335 --> 00:11:30,610
real work. Rules come out of friction, not

272
00:11:30,610 --> 00:11:31,980
out of sitting and thinking.

273
00:11:33,348 --> 00:11:35,185
The episode started by asking what

274
00:11:35,185 --> 00:11:37,760
Claude.md is, and it ends on the same

275
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:41,005
question. A document written for an AI.

276
00:11:41,748 --> 00:11:43,560
When should it be short, and when may it

277
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:46,360
be long? The answer is not in the word

278
00:11:46,360 --> 00:11:49,235
count. It is in when the document gets

279
00:11:49,235 --> 00:11:52,210
read. Something that is always loaded pays

280
00:11:52,210 --> 00:11:54,580
the standing cost, so keep it short.

281
00:11:55,323 --> 00:11:57,385
Something that loads on demand pays only

282
00:11:57,385 --> 00:11:59,555
when it is needed, so make it enough.

283
00:12:00,298 --> 00:12:02,510
Once you see that it is context, charged

284
00:12:02,510 --> 00:12:03,555
for on every turn,

285
00:12:03,623 --> 00:12:05,005
everything else follows.

286
00:12:06,392 --> 00:12:08,842
So far, what we have is a pile of finished

287
00:12:08,842 --> 00:12:11,387
entries, sitting in a folder one by one.

288
00:12:12,129 --> 00:12:14,517
Next episode we turn that pile into a real

289
00:12:14,517 --> 00:12:17,254
knowledge base. Entries linked to each

290
00:12:17,254 --> 00:12:19,442
other, an index you can search, and

291
00:12:19,442 --> 00:12:21,417
questions that get answers across several

292
00:12:21,417 --> 00:12:24,187
entries. See you next time.
